    "grade": "10-12",
    "points": 5,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "The fact that $5=2+3$ shows that some prime numbers can be written as the sum of two other prime numbers. How many prime numbers can be written as the sum of two prime numbers in two different ways? ($2+3$ and $3+2$ are not considered different).",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "0"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "1"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "2"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "3"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "more than 3"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "A",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Parity",
        "body": "A prime $p$ that is a sum of two primes is larger than 2 and so odd. An odd sum of two primes needs one even prime, i.e. the prime 2, and the other is $p-2$. So there is at most one way for each prime. Hence no prime can be written in two different ways.\n\n**Answer: (A) 0**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "number-theory"
    ],
    "difficulty": 2,
    "duplicateOf": null,
    "reviewNote": null,
    "wrongPoints": -1
  },
