    "grade": "10-12",
    "points": 5,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "A farmer has both sheep and chickens. The average number of legs per animal is $\\ell$. The ratio of the number of sheep to the number of chickens is",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "$\\dfrac{\\ell}{3(4-\\ell)}$"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "$\\dfrac{\\ell-2}{4-\\ell}$"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "$\\dfrac{3(\\ell-2)}{\\ell}$"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "$\\dfrac{(\\ell-2)^2}{16-\\ell^2}$"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "$\\dfrac{7(\\ell^2-4)}{5(16-\\ell^2)}$"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Set up an equation",
        "body": "With $s$ sheep and $c$ chickens, $\\ell=\\frac{4s+2c}{s+c}$. So $\\ell s+\\ell c=4s+2c$, i.e. $(4-\\ell)s=(\\ell-2)c$ and $\\frac sc=\\frac{\\ell-2}{4-\\ell}$.\n\n**Answer: (B)**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Check with a value",
        "body": "If $\\ell=3$ the animals are half sheep and half chickens, ratio $1$; (B) gives $\\frac{1}{1}=1$, while (A) gives $1$ too but (A) at $\\ell=2$ gives $\\frac{2}{6}\\ne0$ (all chickens means ratio $0$) and (B) gives $0$. Only (B) fits both checks.\n\n**Answer: (B)**"
      }
    ],
