      {
        "title": "Isosceles triangles",
        "body": "Join $AE$ and $ED$. Since $DE=DC$ (radii of the second circle), $\\angle CED=\\theta$. The exterior angle gives $\\angle EDA=2\\theta$. Since $EA=ED$ (radii of the first circle), $\\angle DAE=2\\theta$. Since $EA=EB$, $\\angle EAB=63^\\circ$. Then $\\angle BAC=63^\\circ+2\\theta$ and the angles of triangle $ABC$ give $63^\\circ+(63^\\circ+2\\theta)+\\theta=180^\\circ$, so $3\\theta=54^\\circ$ and $\\theta=18^\\circ$.\n\n**Answer: (A) $18^\\circ$**"
